\details{
\itemize{
\item A trajectory of the Atari Grand Challenge data set, version 1 (Kurin et
al. 2017, \doi{10.48550/arXiv.1705.10998}): one episode of play, recorded
at 60 frames per second. The data set keeps the trajectories of each
game in a folder of its own (\code{spaceinvaders}, \code{qbert}, \code{mspacman},
\code{pinball}, \code{revenge}); the file does not name its game.
\item A trial of Atari-HEAD (Zhang et al. 2020,
\doi{10.1609/aaai.v34i04.6161}; files at \doi{10.5281/zenodo.3451402}).
The game waited for the player in every frame, and the table stores how
long each frame was shown. The archive of each game holds the text files
of its trials beside the archives of their screen images, and a trial
can be read out of it: \code{read_movie("breakout.zip", member = ...)}, with
the members that \code{\link[=movie_members]{movie_members()}} lists. The file \code{meta_data.csv} of
the data set names the game of each trial.
}

Each row holds the action of its frame as one of the 18 action numbers of
the Arcade Learning Environment. These are the states of one joystick, so
\code{\link[=player]{player()}} returns its five controls: the four directions and the fire
button, under the IDs an Atari 2600 joystick has in every other
recording.

\code{\link[=analog_channels]{analog_channels()}} returns the action number as the table writes it
(\code{action}) and, for a trial, how long each frame was shown
(\code{duration_ms}, the column \code{duration(ms)}). A field that the table gives
as \code{null} is \code{NA}; the last row of a trial has no action, so that no
control is held in it. \code{\link[=observation_time]{observation_time()}} of a trial is the time at
which each frame appeared, the sum of the durations of the frames before
it. \code{movie$timing} holds the frame numbers of a trajectory (\code{frame}) or
the frame identifiers of a trial (\code{frame_id}), and
\code{movie$header$trajectoryId} the number in the first line of a
trajectory.

Only the input is read. The tables also give states of the game (the
score, the reward of each frame, the episode and whether it ended) and,
for a trial, the gaze positions of each frame: the points on the screen
at which the player was looking, measured by an eye tracker. These are
not returned; \code{read.csv()} reads them from the same files.

\code{recording_info(movie)$format} says which table was read: \code{"atari_gc"} or
\code{"atari_head"}. A text file that is neither is refused.
